**********Source Check**********

The claim 'The zero-shot performance of **something** on mammogram interpretation tasks was evaluated.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The content explicitly states: "To assessthecapabilitiesofexistingLVLMsininterpretingmammograms,weselect9modelspre-trainedonlarge-scaledatasets.Thesemodels,withsimilarscalesanddis-tinctcharacteristics,havegainedwidespreadrecognitionfortheirperformance.Weconductzero-shotexperimentsontheMammoVQAbenchmarktoevaluatetheirabilitytoscreenandinterpretmammograms." This shows that the zero-shot performance of 9 LVLMs was evaluated on mammogram interpretation tasks. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'The medical VQA paradigm offers a practical framework to test whether general-purpose multimodal models can **in a certain way**.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The content explicitly mentions that MammoVQA, a medical VQA benchmark, provides an "evaluation framework for assessing LVLMs' mammogram screening capabilities." Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) are a type of general-purpose multimodal model, and "mammogram screening capabilities" refers to the "certain way" they are tested. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'The performance improvements **in a certain state** show a promising trend for general large language models (LLMs) to assist with mammography VQA tasks.' is NOT supported by the citation.
<reject> The content explicitly states that systematic evaluation of general large vision-language models (LVLMs), which include large language models (LLMs), reveals their diagnostic performance for mammography VQA tasks is "statistically equivalent to random guessing" and highlights their "unreliability for clinical breast cancer screening." While two specific general models (LLaVA-NeXT-interleave and MedDr) show some advantages in certain tasks, the overall conclusion is that they "still exhibit insufficient accuracy in most tasks, falling short of clinical requirements." This contradicts the statement that performance improvements in a certain state show a promising trend for general LLMs.</reject>

